<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Vollkorn, for names, screen titles and quantities.

  A text serif rather than another grotesque. The previous pairing set a
  "designy" grotesque over a neutral one, which is the default any type-picker
  lands on and is why the screens read as generic however carefully they were
  laid out. A recipe title belongs in a serif for the same reason it does in a
  cookbook, and a serif over a grotesque is the pairing food editorial has used
  for a century.

  Vollkorn is German for wholemeal, which is a better reason to pick a font for
  a pantry than most. It has ink in it: sturdy, slightly rough, and its figures
  carry enough weight to head a column of quantities.

  Cut one step heavier than it was: bold for the names, extrabold for the page
  title, bold italic for the notes. At the old semibold the writing sat too
  lightly against ruling this dark, and a list written in pen is heavier than
  the lines it is written on. Going a further step to 800/900 was tried and
  rejected: the names grew wide enough to leave the dot leaders with nothing
  to run through.

  The declared weights below are 600 and 700 because that is what Android
  matches textStyle against, not a claim about how the faces are drawn. A plain
  TextView asks for 400 and a bold one asks for 700, so the two entries have to
  straddle those requests for both to be reachable at all.
-->
<font-family xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <font
        android:font="@font/vollkorn_bold"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="600" />
    <font
        android:font="@font/vollkorn_extrabold"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="700" />
    <!-- The italic is for notes: the thing someone adds to a list afterwards,
         in the margin or under the line, rather than part of the list itself. -->
    <font
        android:font="@font/vollkorn_bold_italic"
        android:fontStyle="italic"
        android:fontWeight="600" />
</font-family>
